{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# One-hot forwarding bypass\n\n## Changes\n\n- `forward_unit` now emits three one-hot selects per source operand: bit 0\n  selects the ID/EX register-file value, bit 1 selects EX/MEM, and bit 2\n  selects MEM/WB.\n- EX/MEM matches take priority over MEM/WB matches. Writes to x0 are ignored;\n  when neither producer matches, the register-file value is selected.\n- `ex_stage` forms both operands by AND-masking each source and OR-combining\n  the masked values. `core.sv` wires the widened select signals.\n- Added a guarded `core_pkg.sv` include in `core.sv` so the requested lexical\n  wildcard Verilator lint command sees the shared typedefs before their use.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Verilator lint: pass with the requested wildcard command.\n- Formal: pass, 53 checks and 0 failures, using the automatically selected\n  single-issue wrapper for `nret: 1`.\n- Cosim: pass for selftest and CoreMark, including random instruction and data\n  memory stalls. The Makefile build needed `NRET=1`; without it, its default\n  `NRET=2` tries to compile channel-1 ports that this `nret: 1` core does not\n  expose.\n- Gowin timing, placement option 0: Fmax 5.355 MHz. The worst path still runs\n  from the EX/MEM destination register through forwarding and EX/ALU to the\n  data-memory address (192.036 ns, 150 cells). This single-placement result\n  does not establish a fitness improvement across the evaluator's placements.\n\n## Concerns\n\nThe new mux is functionally validated, but the measured worst path still\ncrosses the forwarding unit and ALU, and its reported depth remains high.\nThe placement-option-0 timing result alone does not show a performance gain.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
