{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && git diff --check\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n# Implementation notes: synchronous register-file reads\n\n## Changes\n\n- Replaced the asynchronous dual-read register file with two identical\n  storage copies, one per registered read port. Both copies retain x0,\n  reset-to-zero, synchronous WB writes, and same-edge WB collision bypass.\n- Registered fetched instruction, PC, and validity in IF. IF holds that\n  bundle across downstream stalls, discards it on redirects, and inserts a\n  bubble when imem does not accept a request.\n- Moved the register-file read edge to the same edge that ID captures the\n  decoded metadata. The top-level EX input combines the registered controls\n  and registered operands, keeping them aligned while allowing the sampled\n  operands to observe writes closer to issue.\n- Updated hazard handling for the fetch/read boundary. Load-use and divider\n  holds preserve the accepted fetch, and dmem holds reread the held EX\n  instruction's sources. Redirects still override fetch stalls.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\n- The read is sampled from the registered IF/ID instruction while ID\n  captures its controls, rather than sampling directly from the live imem\n  bus. This avoids reading operands before older WB updates become visible.\n- The one-placement timing run reported 49.244 MHz, 14 logic levels,\n  2430 logic cells, 612 registers, 8 LUT-RAMs, 6 BSRAMs, and 6 DSPs. The\n  worst path now starts at an EX/MEM or MEM/WB destination register and\n  passes through forwarding and the EX ALU to the data-memory address. The\n  current timing result does not show an Fmax gain over the stored historical\n  `core.yaml` value of 50.738 MHz; the evaluation flow uses multiple\n  placement options for its final score.\n- The default `make cosim` command selected NRET=2, which cannot compile\n  against this core's NRET=1 interface. Running it with `NRET=1` matched the\n  declared contract and passed both ELF checks.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint: passed with the requested command.\n- Formal: passed, 53 checks and 0 failures. During development, the register\n  check first exposed a stale prefetched operand across a hold, then a read\n  sampled before an older writeback. Moving the synchronous read to coincide\n  with the ID metadata capture resolved both cases.\n- Cosim: passed with `NRET=1`; 2 ELF checks passed (selftest and CoreMark).\n- Gowin timing: completed; worst path is the forwarding mux through EX ALU\n  to dmem address, at 49.244 MHz for the reported placement option.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
