{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"test -f cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Implementation notes\n\n## Changes\n\n- `rtl/divider.sv` now captures the final quotient or remainder in `result_q` on the terminal iteration and raises a registered one-cycle `done_q` pulse. The iterative next-state arithmetic no longer drives `done` or the divider result output combinationally.\n- `rtl/core.sv` gates `div_start` with `!div_done` so the held DIV/REM cannot restart while the registered completion pulse releases it.\n\nThe implementation follows the hypothesis. The completion path adds one cycle after the 32nd divider step.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint command from the task: passed with no warnings.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: passed, 53 checks and 0 failures.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl OBJ_DIR=cores/bench/obj_dir NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: passed, both ELF programs tested.\n- Bare `make cosim TARGET=bench` initially failed at C++ compile because the build script defaults to NRET=2 while this core declares NRET=1. Rerunning with the core's configured NRET=1 passed.\n- `make timing TARGET=bench`: place option 0 reported 45.907 MHz, 2,563 logic cells, and 633 registers. The listed worst path still traverses EX, the divider, fetch, hazard, and decode. The run therefore does not show a timing improvement on that path; placement variation and the full evaluator flow remain unmeasured.\n\n## Concerns\n\nThe added result register increases register use, and the local timing report still identifies a path through the divider-to-hazard region. Functional checks pass, but the timing result should be judged against the evaluator's multi-placement run.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
