{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 8 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && git status --short'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261001-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple stores with a one-entry buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark injects dmem stalls on roughly 22% of cycles, and the current\\npipeline freezes behind every stalled store. A store can wait for the bus\\nindependently of the next arithmetic instructions, so this stall need not\\nimmediately stop issue. The existing critical control path also crosses\\nmemory backpressure logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The benchmark injects dmem stalls on roughly 22% of cycles, and the current\\npipeline freezes behind every stalled store. A store can wait for the bus\\nindependently of the next arithmetic instructions, so this stall need not\\nimmediately stop issue. The existing critical control path also crosses\\nmemory backpressure logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry posted-store buffer at the MEM boundary. When a store reaches\\nMEM and the buffer is empty, let the pipeline advance while the buffer holds\\nits aligned address, replicated write data, and byte enables until dmemReady\\naccepts the request. Keep the buffered request stable under backpressure.\\nStall a later store only when the buffer is occupied, and hold a load at MEM\\nuntil all older buffered stores drain so memory ordering and load-after-store\\nbehavior remain precise. This should hide isolated store wait cycles behind\\nindependent execution, improving CoreMark iterations per cycle without\\nadding lookup logic to the fetch or ALU paths.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry pending-store buffer, stable bus request selection, enqueue/dequeue handling, and load ordering interlock.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the MEM buffer's backpressure and ordering status to pipeline stall control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Advance independent instructions behind a buffered store while holding accesses that cannot yet be accepted safely.\"}]}, \"\n…[26058 of 29130 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8dad3e5b88888f4d]…\nID combinational bundle and ID/EX register; no separate prediction stage or table was added.\\n- The fetch target adder adds area and could affect timing. Gowin measured 43.874 MHz median Fmax, 2694 logic cells, and 631 registers. The critical path reported by placement 0 still runs through register-file and EX logic.\\n- This core declares `nret: 1`, while the local Make targets default the cosim build macro to NRET=2. I ran the checks with `NRET=1`; no build or harness files were changed.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- Verilator lint: pass.\\n- Formal: pass, 53 checks, using `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` (which auto-selects the single-issue wrapper/config).\\n- Cosim: pass, 5 ELF runs, with and without memory stalls, using `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`.\\n- FPGA evaluation: pass. CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 4,442,158 cycles with the expected validation output; reported fitness was 98.77 iter/s.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-01T05:29:22.893765Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
