{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n -C 2 'hyp-20261001-002-r12s1|Synchronous register-file read stage' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && git diff --stat && git log -5 --oneline -- cores/bench/rtl\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r1s0 (regression, +2.97%): Explicit one-hot forwarding with 32-bit mask-and-OR operand logic regressed fitness, so removing binary select encoding alone did not offset the added datapath logic on this target.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +787.71%): Moving DIV/REM out of the combinational ALU into a shared iterative divider removes their long arithmetic cones from ordinary EX timing; the in-order interlock confines the 32-step latency to divide instructions.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.44%): Static backward prediction puts branch decode, immediate assembly, and a PC-relative adder on the IF next-PC path, so any saved EX redirect bubbles must outweigh the fetch-path cost on CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r2s0 (regression, -9.03%): Widening each restoring-step subtraction to 34 bits to extract borrow regressed fitness, showing that compare/subtract fusion can lengthen the divider carry path when the target does not map the wider arithmetic efficiently.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r2s1 (regression, -3.53%): A 33-bit non-restoring step still puts sign-controlled divisor inversion and carry propagation on the divider recurrence, while final remainder correction adds logic, so removing the per-step compare did not improve this target.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.65%): Even an 8-entry tagged BTB puts indexed tag/counter reads, tag comparison, and a 32-bit target mux on the IF PC update path, so lookup timing can outweigh saved EX redirect bubbles on this FPGA.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 (regression, -10.74%): Even a shallow return stack with a narrow `JALR x0,x1,0` decode adds fetch-side selection cost, and CoreMark's saved return redirects did not repay it on this FPGA.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r4s2 (regression, -24.60%): Operand-based ID branch resolution can trade EX redirect bubbles for a longer ID-to-fetch-PC path, since EX/MEM bypass muxes, branch comparison, and target generation now feed the red\n…[3426 of 6498 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:feba7c6f34889332]…\nct-JAL prediction puts instruction decode, immediate assembly, and a PC-relative adder on the IF-to-PC path, while carrying and checking `predicted_next_pc` adds pipeline cost; skipping jump redirects did not repay that overhead on CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r11s0 (regression, -2.01%): The new init cycle adds latency to every signed DIV/REM with a negative operand, so launch-path retiming must earn back that per-operation cost in measured timing on CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r11s1 (broken, n/a): Splitting EX adds another producer stage, so forwarding and RAW interlocks must cover the operand register as well as EX/MEM; riscv-formal's reg check exposed a stale source value after the retime.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r11s2 (regression, -11.08%): Registering a one-entry branch-tag lookup off the PC feedback path still carries a 32-bit predicted target through IF/ID and ID/EX and adds EX mismatch recovery, whose pipeline and control costs outweighed saved loop redirects on CoreMark.\n", "exit_code": 1, "status": "failed"}}
