{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\")];\nfor r in rows:\n if r.get(\\\"id\\\") in (\\\"hyp-20261001-001-r4s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20261001-001-r7s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20261001-001-r10s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20261001-002-r10s1\\\"):\n  print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\",r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\"),\\\"\\\\nPATH\\\",r.get(\\\"critical_path\\\"))'\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261001-001-r4s0 Bank the register-file read mux \n {'motivation': 'Recent timing reports repeatedly start at a register-file storage bit and end at an ID/EX operand register. The asynchronous variable-index reads and write-first bypasses sit directly on this operand capture path.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Replace each flat 32-entry asynchronous read with a four-bank, eight-register mux: select within each bank using the low address bits, then select a bank using the high bits. Keep x0 reads and same-cycle WB write-first bypass behavior exact. A staged mux topology may map to shallower local LUT logic and reduce read-select fanout, improving Fmax without adding a pipeline cycle or changing instruction timing.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 6, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/reg_file.sv', 'description': 'Use a four-bank hierarchical read mux for both register-file ports while preserving x0 and write-first bypass semantics.'}]} \n  \nPATH {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs1_val_3_s9', 'slack_ns': -15.529, 'arrival_ns': 25.824, 'logic_cells': 40, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_divider', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_rf']}\n\n hyp-20261001-001-r7s0 Bypass ALU selection for memory addresses \n {'motivation': 'Recent timing reports repeatedly place the critical endpoint in the EX ALU, including the data-memory address path. A load or store always adds its forwarded base register to its immediate, but currently passes through the general ALU operand selection and operation-result selection used by every instruction.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Add a dedicated effective-address sum from the already-forwarded rs1 value and the decoded immediate, and select that sum into EX/MEM for loads and stores. This removes the AUIPC/operand muxes and general ALU result selection from the memory-address path while leaving the existing ALU operations and their results unchanged for non-memory instructions. The extra adder uses available LUT headroom and targets the repeatedl\n…[2846 of 5918 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f9fb77e5a67ba573]…\ne in-order retirement while removing the divider wait signal from the ID/EX hold mux, which should shorten the current critical control path.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 7, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Coordinate divider active state with fetch hold and decode bubble insertion while preserving older pipeline progress.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Capture DIV/REM instruction metadata and operands in a dedicated execution context, then emit its EX/MEM result on completion.'}, {'file': 'rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'description': 'Hold fetch and inject a decode bubble during detached divider execution without holding ID/EX for the full operation.'}]} \n  \nPATH {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs1_val_0_s100', 'slack_ns': -15.791, 'arrival_ns': 26.085, 'logic_cells': 47, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_divider', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
