{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r15s0.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20261001-001-r15s0\ntitle: \\\"Narrow the divider iteration counter\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The shared restoring divider processes exactly 32 quotient bits, but its iteration counter is six bits wide. The extra counter bit adds state and participates in increment and terminal-count logic on the divider control path, which contributes to the recurring divider-to-fetch timing cone.\nhypothesis: |\n  Reduce the divider iteration counter to five bits while retaining the existing count-through-31 schedule and completion behavior. This removes the unused high counter state and simplifies its increment and equality logic, potentially improving divider control timing and placement without changing instruction latency or arithmetic results.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/divider.sv\n    description: \\\"Use a five-bit iteration counter for the divider's 32-step schedule and preserve completion on iteration 31.\\\"\nEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
