{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r1s0 (regression, +2.97%): Explicit one-hot forwarding with 32-bit mask-and-OR operand logic regressed fitness, so removing binary select encoding alone did not offset the added datapath logic on this target.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +787.71%): Moving DIV/REM out of the combinational ALU into a shared iterative divider removes their long arithmetic cones from ordinary EX timing; the in-order interlock confines the 32-step latency to divide instructions.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.44%): Static backward prediction puts branch decode, immediate assembly, and a PC-relative adder on the IF next-PC path, so any saved EX redirect bubbles must outweigh the fetch-path cost on CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r2s0 (regression, -9.03%): Widening each restoring-step subtraction to 34 bits to extract borrow regressed fitness, showing that compare/subtract fusion can lengthen the divider carry path when the target does not map the wider arithmetic efficiently.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r2s1 (regression, -3.53%): A 33-bit non-restoring step still puts sign-controlled divisor inversion and carry propagation on the divider recurrence, while final remainder correction adds logic, so removing the per-step compare did not improve this target.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.65%): Even an 8-entry tagged BTB puts indexed tag/counter reads, tag comparison, and a 32-bit target mux on the IF PC update path, so lookup timing can outweigh saved EX redirect bubbles on this FPGA.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 (regression, -10.74%): Even a shallow return stack with a narrow `JALR x0,x1,0` decode adds fetch-side selection cost, and CoreMark's saved return redirects did not repay it on this FPGA.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r4s2 (regression, -24.60%): Operand-based ID branch resolution can trade EX redirect bubbles for a longer ID-to-fetch-PC path, since EX/MEM bypass muxes, branch comparison, and target generation now feed the redirect decision.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r5s0 (regression, -12.18%): Explicitly staging each async register-file read as eight replicated 4:1 selections followed by an 8:1 mux worsened fitness, suggesting the low-address select fanout and routing can outweigh local mux sizing on this FPGA.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r5s1 (regression, +0.49%): A four-entry instruction queue yielded only a marginal CoreMark gain, suggesting randomized imem gaps do not overlap enough backend wait in this in-order core to repay enqueue, consume, and redirect-flush control.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r5s2 (regression, -2.37%): Registering next-PC BTB predictions moves tag and target lookup off the PC feedback path, but the 16-entry table, EX mismatch checks, and training/recovery logic still did not repay their cost through fewer CoreMark loop redirects.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
