{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r6s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261001-001-r6s0\ntitle: \"Mask uninitialized register-file storage\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The register-file read path repeatedly appears in timing reports, and its\n  storage array currently receives a synchronous reset across all 32 words.\n  That reset can prevent compact distributed-memory inference and adds reset\n  control to the storage datapath.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the register-file's reset-to-zero behavior with a resettable valid bit\n  for each nonzero register, while removing reset from the data array itself.\n  Reads of invalid entries return zero, writes set the corresponding valid bit,\n  and the existing x0 and write-first bypass rules remain unchanged. This may\n  let Gowin map the two asynchronous read ports to distributed memory while\n  reducing reset fanout and improving the register-file-to-ID timing path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Replace per-word data reset with resettable valid bits while preserving zero-after-reset, x0, and write-first read behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
