ds a same-cycle WB tag\n  comparison and 32-bit bypass mux to both asynchronous read ports.\nhypothesis: |\n  Remove the WB write-through muxes from the register-file read ports and\n  retain the most recent WB destination and value in a small pipeline\n  register. Add that retained value as the lowest-priority EX forwarding\n  source, below current EX/MEM and MEM/WB writers. This retimes the bypass\n  across the ID/EX boundary, shortening the register-file-to-ID path while\n  preserving the value seen by instructions that read a register on the\n  cycle it is written. The added forwarding source may lengthen EX, but the\n  current timing history points to the read and forwarding path as the main\n  bottleneck.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: Remove same-cycle WB comparison and write-through muxing from both asynchronous register-file read ports.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Register the current WB write tag and value for use by EX in the following cycle.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: Add a lowest-priority forwarding hit for the retained prior-cycle WB destination.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Select the retained prior-cycle WB value when neither EX/MEM nor current MEM/WB has the freshest operand.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
