{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n  mot=(d.get('motivation') or '')\n  hyp=d.get('hypothesis') or {}\n  if isinstance(hyp,dict): mot += ' '+hyp.get('hypothesis','')\n  print('\\\\n'+d.get('id','')+' '+d.get('title','')+' ['+str(d.get('delta_pct'))+']')\n  print(mot[:900].replace('\\\\n',' '))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nbaseline-bench-a1886e3 Baseline retest for cores/bench [0.0]\n \n\nhyp-20261001-001-r1s0 One-hot forwarding bypass [2.97]\nThe recorded worst path runs from a MEM/WB destination register through the forwarding unit and EX ALU to the data-memory address. Binary forwarding selection adds selector encoding and data-mux logic to this path.  Generate priority-correct one-hot hits for the register-file, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB operands, then combine masked operand values in EX with an OR. This removes the binary select decode between register-tag comparison and ALU input, which may shorten the critical forwarding-to-address path. \n\nhyp-20261001-001-r2s0 Fuse divider compare and subtract [-9.03]\nThe measured critical path now crosses the shared iterative divider. Each restoring step separately compares the shifted remainder against the divisor and subtracts that same divisor, creating redundant arithmetic in the timed datapath.  Form one widened trial difference between the shifted remainder and divisor, then use its borrow bit both to decide whether to accept the subtraction and to update the quotient bit. This replaces the separate magnitude comparator and subtractor with one arithmetic cone and should reduce divider delay and logic area without changing the 32-step algorithm or RV32M results. \n\nhyp-20261001-001-r3s0 Share the RV32M multiplier [-9.74]\nThe current ALU describes three separate 64-bit multiplier expressions for signed, unsigned, and mixed-sign high products. Sharing a single unsigned product can reduce DSP use and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K, which may improve placement and the clock limit even though the current reported worst path crosses the divider and stall-control logic.  Compute one unsigned 32-by-32 product for all multiply instructions. MUL uses its low word directly; MULHU uses its high word directly; MULHSU and MULH correct that high word by subtracting the appropriate sign-dependent operand terms. This preserves single-cycle multiply behavior and RV32M results while removing redundant multi\n…[3117 of 6189 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:10c256243cdcff5e]…\nadder uses available LUT headroom and targets the repeatedly observed EX-to-address timing bottleneck without changing pipeline latency or memory semantics. \n\nhyp-20261001-001-r8s0 Retiming writeback operand bypass [0.85]\nRecent timing paths repeatedly include register-file reads feeding EX and address generation. The register file currently adds a same-cycle WB tag comparison and 32-bit bypass mux to both asynchronous read ports.  Remove the WB write-through muxes from the register-file read ports and retain the most recent WB destination and value in a small pipeline register. Add that retained value as the lowest-priority EX forwarding source, below current EX/MEM and MEM/WB writers. This retimes the bypass across the ID/EX boundary, shortening the register-file-to-ID path while preserving the value seen by instructions that read a register on the cycle it is written. The added forwarding source may lengthen EX, but the current timing history points to the read and forwarding path as the main bottleneck. \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
