{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r9s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261001-001-r9s0\ntitle: \"Segmented carry-select ALU adder\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  Recent timing reports repeatedly place the critical path from register-file operands through the EX ALU to the data-memory address. Address generation uses a 32-bit addition, and the same ALU addition is also common in CoreMark's integer instruction stream.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the ALU's monolithic 32-bit addition with a segmented carry-select implementation that computes small upper blocks for both possible carry-in values and selects each block once the lower carry is known. This reduces serial carry propagation on ADD and load/store effective-address operations without adding pipeline latency or changing instruction behavior; it targets the repeated EX-to-memory timing path while avoiding extra full-width arithmetic units.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Implement ALU_ADD with small carry-select blocks to shorten the 32-bit addition path used for effective addresses and integer adds.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
