etween register-tag comparison and ALU input, which may shorten the critical forwarding-to-address path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Produce one-hot operand-source selects while preserving EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority and x0 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the binary case forwarding muxes with one\n…[92858 of 95930 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:17a486854dd863c3]…\nrt mux but adds a retained 32-bit value, another forwarding compare/select, and stall-tracking state, so relocating the bypass did not pay for its added EX and control logic.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r8s1 (regression, -0.90%): A one-entry registered IF/ID boundary keeps issue single-width but adds payload flops and redirect/stall coordination, and this CoreMark regression shows fetch/decode retiming alone did not repay those costs here.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r9s0 (regression, -3.39%): Four 8-bit carry-select blocks duplicate upper `a+b` and `a+b+1` arithmetic yet retain a block-to-block carry selection chain, so extra muxing and routing can outweigh reduced bit-level carry depth on the ALU effective-address path.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r9s1 (regression, -9.12%): Sharing RV32M operations through one unsigned product reduces DSP use, but signed high-word correction and subtraction logic can lengthen the ALU result path enough that the timing loss outweighs the hardware savings on this FPGA.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
