{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261001-002-r3s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261001-002-r4s1\\\") | [.id,.title,(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis),(.hypothesis.motivation // \\\"\\\"),(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")] | @json' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"[\"hyp-20261001-002-r3s1\",\"Register divider completion and result\",\"Retiming the shared divider interface to capture its final quotient or remainder in a result register and assert a registered one-cycle done pulse will separate the iterative arithmetic recurrence from global pipeline release. Gate divider start while that completion pulse is active to avoid restarting the held DIV/REM instruction. This adds at most one cycle to divide operations, while shortening the divider-to-hazard-to-decode path that determines Fmax.\\n\",\"The measured critical path still crosses the iterative divider, fetch, and hazard logic before returning to decode. The divider's terminal-count test and live result currently feed completion and pipeline-release control combinationally, putting arithmetic state on a wide control path that affects ordinary pipeline timing.\\n\",\"\"]\n[\"hyp-20261001-002-r4s1\",\"Decouple stores with a one-entry buffer\",\"Add a one-entry posted-store buffer at the MEM boundary. When a store reaches\\nMEM and the buffer is empty, let the pipeline advance while the buffer holds\\nits aligned address, replicated write data, and byte enables until dmemReady\\naccepts the request. Keep the buffered request stable under backpressure.\\nStall a later store only when the buffer is occupied, and hold a load at MEM\\nuntil all older buffered stores drain so memory ordering and load-after-store\\nbehavior remain precise. This should hide isolated store wait cycles behind\\nindependent execution, improving CoreMark iterations per cycle without\\nadding lookup logic to the fetch or ALU paths.\\n\",\"The benchmark injects dmem stalls on roughly 22% of cycles, and the current\\npipeline freezes behind every stalled store. A store can wait for the bus\\nindependently of the next arithmetic instructions, so this stall need not\\nimmediately stop issue. The existing critical control path also crosses\\nmemory backpressure logic.\\n\",\"\"]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
