ain, suggesting randomized imem gaps do not overlap enough backend wait in this in-order core to repay enqueue, consume, and redirect-flush control.\nhyp-20261001-002-r6s1 | regression | Δ -11.66 | Pipeline the multiplier partial products | Fmax 43.578 | path cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0 -> cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_8_s1\n  - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.66%): A shared two-stage multiplier holds this in-order EX stage for an extra cycle on each multiply, so its per-operation latency can outweigh shorter multiplier logic on CoreMark.\nhyp-20261001-002-r7s1 | regression | Δ\n…[1466 of 4538 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:15534edb55454f6c]…\n001-002-r10s1 (improvement, +4.99%): Capturing forwarded operands and the full ID/EX payload in an EX-local divider context lets ID/EX bubble without divider wait in its hold mux; holding fetch through completion preserves in-order issue while improving fitness.\nhyp-20261001-002-r11s1 | broken | Δ None | Split the execute stage | Fmax None | path None -> None\n  - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r11s1 (broken, n/a): Splitting EX adds another producer stage, so forwarding and RAW interlocks must cover the operand register as well as EX/MEM; riscv-formal's reg check exposed a stale source value after the retime.\nhyp-20261001-002-r12s1 | regression | Δ -3.83 | Synchronous register-file read stage | Fmax 52.2 | path cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_1_s0 -> dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\n  - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r12s1 (regression, -3.83%): A synchronous register-file read adds a fetch/operand alignment boundary with startup, redirect, and stall coordination costs, and that overhead did not repay its hoped-for timing benefit on CoreMark.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r13s1.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20261001-002-r13s1\ntitle: \\\"Split operand forwarding from execute\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The recent timing result puts the critical path from an EX/MEM destination tag through forwarding and the EX ALU into the next memory address. That combines register matching, a 32-bit operand mux, and arithmetic in one cycle. A pipeline boundary between operand selection and execution can shorten this path while retaining one instruction per cycle in the steady state.\nhypothesis: |\n  Divide EX into an operand-capture stage and an execute stage. Resolve and register operands at the new boundary, including a bypass from the concurrently executing older instruction's result and the existing writeback value, so the arithmetic stage does not repeat the long producer comparisons and forwarding muxes. Extend interlocks for loads and other unavailable results, flush the younger captured instruction on redirects, and preserve detached DIV/REM completion and in-order retirement. This should raise Fmax enough to outweigh the added branch recovery latency. The prior split-EX attempt failed its register check because it did not account for dependencies across the new producer stage; the bypass and stall rules must explicitly cover those dependencies.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Add a registered execute-operand payload with source tags and the metadata needed to preserve instruction and RVFI alignment.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Wire the operand and execute stages while preserving the single-channel RVFI contract and detached divider sequencing.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Capture resolved operands before ALU and branch work, provide same-cycle older-result bypass into that capture, and flush or hold the new stage correctly.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Resolve source matches for the new stage boundary and prioritize the concurrent older execute result over writeback data.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Add interlocks for load and other unavailable producer results across the new stage, and coordinate redirect, memory stall, and divider bubbles.\\\"\nEOF\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport yaml\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r13s1.yaml'\nd=yaml.safe_load(open(p))\nprint(d['id'], d['category'], len(d['changes']), d['expected_impact'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
