{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(p)]; [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],r.get(\\\"delta_pct\\\"),r.get(\\\"lesson\\\", \\\"\\\"),\\\"\\\\nHYP:\\\",r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\", \\\"\\\")) for r in rows if r.get(\\\"category\\\")==\\\"predictor\\\"]'\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 Predict backward branches taken 6.44 - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.44%): Static backward prediction puts branch decode, immediate assembly, and a PC-relative adder on the IF next-PC path, so any saved EX redirect bubbles must outweigh the fetch-path cost on CoreMark. \nHYP: Add a static backward-taken predictor for valid conditional branches. In IF,\nrecognize legal branch encodings with a negative B-immediate, form the target,\nand speculatively advance the PC there while carrying the prediction into EX.\nEX compares prediction with the resolved branch and redirects only on a\nmisprediction, using the branch target or fall-through as appropriate. This\nshould eliminate taken-loop redirect bubbles at small area cost; forward\nbranches retain the existing not-taken behavior.\n\n\n hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 Small tagged branch target buffer -7.65 - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.65%): Even an 8-entry tagged BTB puts indexed tag/counter reads, tag comparison, and a 32-bit target mux on the IF PC update path, so lookup timing can outweigh saved EX redirect bubbles on this FPGA. \nHYP: Add an 8-entry direct-mapped, tagged branch target buffer with two-bit direction\ncounters. IF predicts taken branches and jumps from a BTB hit using the\nstored target, keeping branch decode and PC-relative addition out of the\nfetch path; misses predict sequential execution. Train entries in EX from\nresolved branches and jumps, carry the predicted next PC with each fetched\ninstruction, and redirect only when EX finds that the prediction differs\nfrom the actual next PC. The table should capture recurring loop branches,\ncalls, and returns while keeping the lookup small enough to limit Fmax cost.\n\n\n hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 Predict returns with a small return stack -10.74 - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 (regression, -10.74%): Even a shallow return stack with a narrow `JALR x0,x1,0` decode adds fetch-side selection cost, and CoreMark's saved return redirects did not repay it on this FPGA. \nHYP:\n…[5094 of 8166 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:151b5cb49e7b9457]…\nimprove on fixed backward-taken prediction without the area and lookup cost\nof multi-entry direction tables.\n\n\n hyp-20261001-003-r10s2 Predict direct JAL targets in fetch -11.25 - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r10s2 (regression, -11.25%): Even table-free direct-JAL prediction puts instruction decode, immediate assembly, and a PC-relative adder on the IF-to-PC path, while carrying and checking `predicted_next_pc` adds pipeline cost; skipping jump redirects did not repay that overhead on CoreMark. \nHYP: Recognize valid direct JAL instructions in IF, form their PC-relative target,\nand speculatively set the next fetch PC to that target. Carry the predicted\nnext PC with the instruction so EX can suppress its redirect and pipeline\nflush when the resolved target agrees, while retaining normal correction for\nmisaligned targets and leaving conditional branches and JALR on the existing\npath. This removes the two younger-instruction bubbles on direct jumps with\nno direction counter, BTB lookup, or indirect-target state.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
