{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r14s2.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20261001-003-r14s2\ntitle: \\\"Single-entry learned loop predictor\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The existing sequential fetch path loses redirect cycles on taken loops, but prior static, BTB, return-stack, and decode-stage predictors reduced Fmax by adding target generation, table lookup, or a longer fetch-control path. A single learned loop entry can avoid a multi-entry indexed read while targeting a repeatedly executed taken backedge.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-entry predictor for conditional backward branches. When EX resolves a taken backward branch, record its PC and target and initialize a saturating direction counter; update the counter when that same branch resolves again. IF predicts taken only when its PC matches the registered entry and the counter is in a taken state, selecting the cached target without branch decode or immediate addition. Carry the prediction with the instruction and let EX redirect only on a direction misprediction, preserving normal handling for other branches and jumps. The single tag comparison and dedicated target register should cost less timing than a multi-entry BTB while eliminating repeated loop backedge redirects after training.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Carry a predicted-taken bit with the fetched instruction into EX for misprediction recovery.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Add a single registered PC-tag and target lookup that predicts a matching trained loop branch.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Connect EX-resolved taken backward branch training information to the one-entry predictor.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Train the loop entry on resolved backward branches and redirect only when the prediction was incorrect.\\\"\nEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
